1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,160
And now to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,980 --> 00:00:11,780
So I asked a simple question at a troop meeting the other night.

3
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:14,600
I got a kind of a testy response.

4
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:17,160
I had the recorder on.

5
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:19,760
So here's what happened.

6
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:20,880
Where are your badges?

7
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:22,400
Badges?

8
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:24,380
We ain't got no badges.

9
00:00:25,540 --> 00:00:26,600
We don't need no badges.

10
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:29,640
I don't have to show you any stinking badges.

11
00:00:30,460 --> 00:00:36,380
I guess the guys were a little edgy after their long winter vacation came to an end.

12
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:39,760
Well, this is podcast number 52.

13
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:45,720
Hey!

14
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:45,980
Hey!

15
00:00:45,980 --> 00:01:12,780
Well, welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

16
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:13,960
This is Clark Green.

17
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,360
Hey, Humphrey Bogart, eh?

18
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:19,160
Treasurer of the Sierra Madre?

19
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:20,300
Yeah.

20
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:21,920
That's what you just heard.

21
00:01:22,620 --> 00:01:26,320
Does anybody not know that little piece of film trivia?

22
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:27,460
How about that?

23
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:36,860
Well, over the past week, we've gotten lots of messages through the blog on comments and iTunes reviews and emails and things.

24
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:40,040
So let's see what we have to share with you.

25
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:40,880
Here's one.

26
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:44,680
Here's an update on how your podcast is making a positive impact.

27
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:48,260
You recently highlighted the book, Working the Patrol Method.

28
00:01:48,840 --> 00:01:53,460
I ordered six books, enough for me and my patrol assistant scoutmasters.

29
00:01:53,460 --> 00:02:03,280
I gave it to them as Christmas presents and told them I wanted to use it to help us truly adopt the patrol method, to get it written into our troops' DNA.

30
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:07,139
We had a meeting last week and were very excited about the prospects.

31
00:02:07,140 --> 00:02:20,100
Along with helping us evaluate ourselves as leaders and the state of our patrols, we have selected specific application projects motivated by the book that we are working through in this coming month.

32
00:02:20,740 --> 00:02:27,300
My goal is to use the book through this year to help us adults help our boys be great scout patrols.

33
00:02:27,740 --> 00:02:28,980
Hey, it's a process, right?

34
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:34,440
I'm encouraged to use this to improve our process and help us all develop as leaders.

35
00:02:34,859 --> 00:02:37,260
Keep up the good work and thanks for what you do.

36
00:02:37,679 --> 00:02:38,899
And that's from Mike.

37
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:45,480
Well, Mike, I certainly appreciate the kind words and I'm glad that you're finding the patrol method book useful.

38
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:53,540
If you would like to get a look at the book that Mike is talking about, go to scoutmaster.typepad.com.

39
00:02:53,839 --> 00:02:56,120
Look in the right-hand column.

40
00:02:56,120 --> 00:03:05,120
You'll see links to my review of the book, an interview that we did on one of our podcasts, and a link to buy the book.

41
00:03:05,660 --> 00:03:07,580
So thanks again, Mike.

42
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:08,800
Really appreciate it.

43
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:18,460
On iTunes, from another Mike, as a new scoutmaster, this podcast has helped me accelerate my learning and have fun along the way.

44
00:03:18,820 --> 00:03:22,060
I enjoy the jokes, but I'm a scoutmaster, so that probably explains it.

45
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:33,140
The music and the variety of topics that range from BSA history to methods of scouting to camping to teaching scouts to interviews of authors, etc.

46
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:39,660
If you want to enjoy a 25-minute podcast, learn something, and even be challenged, this is the one for you.

47
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:41,340
Well, thanks, Mike, for your kind words.

48
00:03:41,420 --> 00:03:42,500
Certainly appreciate it.

49
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:53,760
Scouter Brian treated the folks at his roundtable recently to CDs that included the Scoutmaster podcast and other podcasts from PTC Media.

50
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:56,460
And PTC Media, it's easy to find on the web.

51
00:03:56,560 --> 00:04:01,360
They've got some excellent podcasts there, and I do encourage you to go over and listen.

52
00:04:02,100 --> 00:04:03,280
Thanks very much, Brian.

53
00:04:03,640 --> 00:04:06,480
Here's a message from Scoutmaster Bill.

54
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:12,100
Bill, this Christmas, when I gave my son a new iPod Touch, he gave me his old iPod Classic.

55
00:04:12,859 --> 00:04:18,219
Now, keep in mind, I've never aspired to own an iPod, so I really don't know anything about him.

56
00:04:18,719 --> 00:04:24,820
That being said, I'd love to get your podcast onto the iPod so I can listen at my leisure without having to use a computer.

57
00:04:25,580 --> 00:04:28,620
So my question is, how do I get the podcast onto the iPod?

58
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:31,680
I know there are some on iTunes, but what about the older podcasts?

59
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:43,820
Well, Bill, what you can do is go to scoutmaster.typepad.com and look for a link to the archive of the Scoutmaster podcast in the upper left-hand column.

60
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:50,520
And that link will lead you to all 51 podcasts that we've done in the past.

61
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:56,040
And as far as how to get them on the iPod, I'd ask your son.

62
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:03,260
When I have a technical problem, I find a scout.

63
00:05:04,740 --> 00:05:07,080
They usually are able to figure it out pretty quick.

64
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:08,220
Here's another message.

65
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:15,920
Dear Scoutmaster, I must say again that your production and delivery are at a par with professional podcasts to which I subscribe.

66
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:17,880
Kudos for the great work you do.

67
00:05:18,440 --> 00:05:19,720
My question is as follows.

68
00:05:19,840 --> 00:05:23,460
Do you wear the campaign hat which is shown in the photograph on the blog?

69
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:26,000
And what make and type of campaign hat?

70
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:27,420
And that comes from Thor.

71
00:05:27,720 --> 00:05:28,460
Hey, thanks, Thor.

72
00:05:28,620 --> 00:05:30,140
And thank you for the kind words.

73
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:34,820
I do wear a campaign hat, but just, you know, at summer camp, but at retreat ceremonies.

74
00:05:34,900 --> 00:05:35,600
That's about it.

75
00:05:35,780 --> 00:05:39,820
I got mine from BSA Supply like 20 years ago.

76
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:41,960
That would be the first place that I would look.

77
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:44,020
They're pretty decent hats.

78
00:05:44,960 --> 00:05:48,520
Mine's lasted that long, even though it doesn't get worn all that often.

79
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:49,520
Fun fact.

80
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:50,520
Fun fact.

81
00:05:50,520 --> 00:05:51,520
Fun fact.

82
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:55,680
The picture in the headline banner for the blog?

83
00:05:55,680 --> 00:05:56,680
Not me.

84
00:05:56,680 --> 00:06:00,919
It's a friend of mine who I snapped a picture of at a retreat ceremony.

85
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:05,880
And it just turned out to fit in what I wanted to do on the banner there.

86
00:06:06,080 --> 00:06:07,200
So now you know.

87
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:07,240
So now you know.

88
00:06:07,640 --> 00:06:11,720
Scott, a Scoutmaster from Loudon, New Hampshire, wrote, thank you for the podcast.

89
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:16,040
I haven't heard many of them yet, but I do appreciate the ones that I have heard.

90
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:19,780
I will go listen as time permits and check out more of them.

91
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:23,360
It's nice to know that a Scoutmaster is not alone in his challenges.

92
00:06:23,540 --> 00:06:25,660
Well, Scott, you are correct.

93
00:06:25,660 --> 00:06:31,980
Most of the problems, difficulties, challenges, and a lot of the fun that we encounter is pretty old hat.

94
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:34,160
Yeah, people have been through it before.

95
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:37,220
So that's why we're here, trying to lend a bit of a hand.

96
00:06:37,920 --> 00:06:43,560
Last time on Podcast 51, we talked about an eagle ambush.

97
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:49,040
And if you didn't listen to that, what happened was is I got an email from a parent who was concerned

98
00:06:49,040 --> 00:06:53,200
that the Scoutmaster in his son's troop was kind of making up rules,

99
00:06:53,200 --> 00:06:56,960
and these rules were preventing his son from becoming an Eagle Scout.

100
00:06:57,340 --> 00:07:01,840
Go back to Podcast 51 and listen to that, and it'll fill the story in for you.

101
00:07:02,280 --> 00:07:05,020
And I had a couple of email responses to that.

102
00:07:05,020 --> 00:07:06,820
Mark in San Diego.

103
00:07:07,300 --> 00:07:08,260
San Diego.

104
00:07:08,460 --> 00:07:10,880
S-A-N-D-Y-E-G-G-O.

105
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:16,280
Well, I found out after I looked on Google that that's a nickname for San Diego.

106
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:17,760
How about that?

107
00:07:18,240 --> 00:07:18,480
Hmm.

108
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,520
Sounded like a breakfast tragedy, but yeah.

109
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:23,900
Mark had this to say.

110
00:07:24,380 --> 00:07:24,659
He says,

111
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:27,680
The arbitrary eagle requirement issue isn't really a new one.

112
00:07:28,120 --> 00:07:30,840
I recall a similar issue about 40 years ago.

113
00:07:30,920 --> 00:07:33,080
I was a Scout, and my father was the Scoutmaster.

114
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:39,080
There was a Scout that was prevented from attaining Eagle by an arbitrary rule of some other troop.

115
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:43,800
And I recall hearing my father on the phone talking to the boy's previous Scoutmaster

116
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:50,520
and being kind of angry because not only did the other guy not agree to the importance of following the real requirements for Eagle,

117
00:07:50,660 --> 00:07:55,660
but he wouldn't give my father any of the boy's leadership or advancement information.

118
00:07:56,300 --> 00:07:58,300
The Scout ended up joining our troop.

119
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:04,740
My father made sure our troop leadership got behind this boy to support his Eagle project and attain his award.

120
00:08:05,380 --> 00:08:10,220
It seems that others in the council adult leadership appreciated and respected my father's actions.

121
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:14,420
He was a great man, and I am trying to do my best to follow his example.

122
00:08:14,420 --> 00:08:20,980
Well, Mark, as we said in that podcast, there's a common problem, unfortunately.

123
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:25,320
It's nothing new, as your story evidences.

124
00:08:26,120 --> 00:08:30,620
Walter got in touch about the same subject, and he said,

125
00:08:30,720 --> 00:08:33,680
You know, I think the ambush scout should just change troops immediately.

126
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:36,159
He doesn't have enough time to work this out,

127
00:08:36,320 --> 00:08:40,960
and he's likely to be re-ambushed when it's time for his Scoutmaster conference.

128
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:44,500
You know, Walter, I think I agree.

129
00:08:44,940 --> 00:08:48,560
Walter suggests that a parent call the district executive, explain the problem,

130
00:08:48,980 --> 00:08:50,760
and ask them to recommend a troop.

131
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:52,540
Walter goes on to say,

132
00:08:53,060 --> 00:08:56,840
I disagree that district council and national should stay out of things like this.

133
00:08:57,120 --> 00:09:00,980
I mean, it is the BSA program, and this kind of behavior is wrong,

134
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:03,480
and it gives the whole program a bad name.

135
00:09:04,320 --> 00:09:08,420
This behavior is far too common, and it's kind of a cancer on the BSA.

136
00:09:08,580 --> 00:09:09,760
Well, I'm with you there.

137
00:09:09,760 --> 00:09:11,160
I'm with you there, Walter.

138
00:09:11,439 --> 00:09:15,740
If this doesn't get resolved at the troop level, then a commissioner needs to step in,

139
00:09:16,240 --> 00:09:20,080
and a commissioner is going to carry more weight than a complaining parent.

140
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:25,400
Other organizations don't allow local units to do whatever they want and use the national name.

141
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:29,780
For instance, McDonald's won't let their people just do whatever they want,

142
00:09:30,100 --> 00:09:31,520
and the BSA shouldn't either.

143
00:09:32,220 --> 00:09:38,580
One of the duties of the council is to ensure that each local unit within its territorial area

144
00:09:38,580 --> 00:09:41,560
carries out the general principles of advancement and scouting,

145
00:09:42,040 --> 00:09:45,060
and the council should really not dodge this responsibility.

146
00:09:45,060 --> 00:09:48,859
Well, yeah, Walter, I agree with you to a point.

147
00:09:49,099 --> 00:09:55,459
It's very difficult for our scouting professionals to involve themselves in these troop level disputes

148
00:09:55,459 --> 00:09:57,260
between parents and leaders sometimes.

149
00:09:57,520 --> 00:10:03,079
Perhaps a little more training, a little bit better of a message getting out to scoutmasters

150
00:10:03,079 --> 00:10:05,640
in things like this podcast, I hope,

151
00:10:06,040 --> 00:10:10,459
will prevent this kind of ambush and this kind of drama in the future.

152
00:10:10,460 --> 00:10:12,500
But I do appreciate your thoughts, Walter.

153
00:10:12,700 --> 00:10:15,380
We've got an awful lot to do on the podcast today.

154
00:10:15,820 --> 00:10:22,920
I want to mention that the Scoutmaster newsletter for January is going out about the same time as the podcast does.

155
00:10:23,260 --> 00:10:25,560
And if you haven't signed up for the Scoutmaster newsletter,

156
00:10:26,100 --> 00:10:31,520
go to the blog, scoutmaster.typepad.com, and go ahead and sign up.

157
00:10:31,600 --> 00:10:36,560
You'll find a sign-up block up in the upper right-hand column.

158
00:10:36,560 --> 00:10:42,939
In Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less, we're going to talk about responding to Youth Leader Initiative.

159
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:50,859
And then in our email segment, which will come up after that, we're going to talk about patrols again.

160
00:10:51,540 --> 00:10:58,520
I got a couple of really good responses with some good thoughts and with a couple of questions about the makeup of patrols,

161
00:10:58,619 --> 00:11:01,680
and that will expand on the conversation that we had last time.

162
00:11:02,140 --> 00:11:04,520
Hey, that's a lot to do, so let's get started.

163
00:11:04,520 --> 00:11:08,819
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

164
00:11:15,819 --> 00:11:18,600
So what I'm going to ask you to do is to imagine that you're 13,

165
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:21,000
and you've just been elected to be a patrol leader.

166
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:23,160
What happens next?

167
00:11:24,300 --> 00:11:30,480
What resources do you have to call on to do the job that you've just been elected to perform?

168
00:11:30,480 --> 00:11:35,580
Well, you've probably watched how all this works for a year or two since you've been in Scouts,

169
00:11:35,640 --> 00:11:38,940
and you've got a pretty good idea of what a patrol leader does.

170
00:11:39,380 --> 00:11:40,600
Gets to wear a patch.

171
00:11:40,860 --> 00:11:41,760
That's a big deal.

172
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:49,060
And it's likely that this is the first time you've had this kind of real responsibility,

173
00:11:49,700 --> 00:11:53,340
the first time that people are looking at you for leadership.

174
00:11:53,340 --> 00:12:00,140
See, you don't really have an appreciation of what this means yet.

175
00:12:00,640 --> 00:12:04,580
You certainly don't look at it the same way that your Scoutmaster does.

176
00:12:04,940 --> 00:12:08,440
Probably not even looking at it the same way as your patrol leader does.

177
00:12:09,220 --> 00:12:13,120
In other words, you might not really have much of a clue at all.

178
00:12:13,120 --> 00:12:17,540
Now, you could be a real self-starter with a lot of energy.

179
00:12:18,680 --> 00:12:23,340
And whether it's bravery or just being a little naive, most likely it's a bit of both,

180
00:12:23,420 --> 00:12:25,900
you jump in with both feet and you really get going.

181
00:12:26,240 --> 00:12:28,280
Now, it's likely that you're going to make some mistakes

182
00:12:28,280 --> 00:12:32,780
and that you'll misunderstand something or you'll just plain forget to do something.

183
00:12:32,900 --> 00:12:36,740
Or you may be indecisive or afraid of looking bad in front of your peers.

184
00:12:36,740 --> 00:12:40,580
And that fear might mean that you don't do much of anything at all.

185
00:12:41,320 --> 00:12:43,140
You try to be inconspicuous.

186
00:12:43,240 --> 00:12:43,980
You don't say much.

187
00:12:44,380 --> 00:12:45,400
You keep your head down.

188
00:12:46,100 --> 00:12:52,340
If your troop leaders and the adults involved in your troop work with you and encourage you,

189
00:12:53,080 --> 00:12:57,760
if they're nonjudgmental and kind, you'll begin to gain some confidence.

190
00:12:58,460 --> 00:12:59,500
You'll begin to be a leader.

191
00:13:00,780 --> 00:13:03,140
If these people react to your work negatively,

192
00:13:03,140 --> 00:13:07,900
if they kind of pester you, bug you, make jokes about you,

193
00:13:08,360 --> 00:13:10,560
if they talk to you angrily,

194
00:13:10,699 --> 00:13:13,780
you're not going to develop much as a leader, are you?

195
00:13:14,420 --> 00:13:16,680
You're going to keep your head down

196
00:13:16,680 --> 00:13:21,260
and you're going to hope that this is over with as soon as possible.

197
00:13:21,780 --> 00:13:24,900
I think about this sort of thing as a scoutmaster a lot

198
00:13:24,900 --> 00:13:30,100
and I understand more and more how important my attitude and reactions are

199
00:13:30,100 --> 00:13:31,380
to developing good leaders.

200
00:13:31,380 --> 00:13:36,600
I think that any initiative that a scout shows in the leadership position,

201
00:13:37,180 --> 00:13:40,840
no matter how imperfect or misguided or lackluster

202
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:43,960
or whatever shortcomings we may detect,

203
00:13:44,320 --> 00:13:47,340
should be met with a very positive response,

204
00:13:47,660 --> 00:13:50,020
with an overwhelmingly positive response.

205
00:13:50,020 --> 00:13:55,120
I remember when I was taking a college course in geography some years ago,

206
00:13:55,300 --> 00:13:58,620
the professor asked a question

207
00:13:58,620 --> 00:14:03,600
and that was to explain what is meant by the description vegetation

208
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:07,680
when it relates to geography.

209
00:14:08,680 --> 00:14:11,060
And one of the students, you know,

210
00:14:11,819 --> 00:14:14,100
hesitatingly raised her hand and she said,

211
00:14:14,100 --> 00:14:20,160
isn't that like the kind of vegetables they grow in that part of the world?

212
00:14:21,960 --> 00:14:25,680
Now, a lot of us stifled a pretty big guffaw,

213
00:14:26,220 --> 00:14:31,660
but the professor's expression remained absolutely unchanged.

214
00:14:31,660 --> 00:14:35,360
And she looked at this student and very kindly said,

215
00:14:35,459 --> 00:14:39,500
well, that's close, but it's not precisely what I was looking for.

216
00:14:39,500 --> 00:14:46,020
Now, if the professor had laughed or belittled an honest attempt to answer the question,

217
00:14:46,560 --> 00:14:51,700
that particular student may have never voluntarily responded to a question again.

218
00:14:53,600 --> 00:14:55,600
When our scouts attempt anything,

219
00:14:56,480 --> 00:14:59,100
we should look on it as an expression of initiative

220
00:14:59,100 --> 00:15:00,940
and take the opportunity to build them up.

221
00:15:00,940 --> 00:15:05,140
Sometimes we have really very little bit to go on.

222
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:09,460
Scouts can be very reluctant to step into a position of responsibility

223
00:15:09,460 --> 00:15:13,500
because they've seen how others who did it before them were treated.

224
00:15:14,780 --> 00:15:18,180
If they know that they're going to be treated, as I said before,

225
00:15:19,020 --> 00:15:21,840
non-judgmentally, with some kindness,

226
00:15:22,520 --> 00:15:24,200
with some understanding and empathy,

227
00:15:25,340 --> 00:15:27,900
they're going to be very anxious to be leaders.

228
00:15:27,900 --> 00:15:31,520
If they think that they're going to be criticized,

229
00:15:32,260 --> 00:15:33,680
they're going to be spoken down to,

230
00:15:34,220 --> 00:15:36,579
and that they're going to receive a general attitude

231
00:15:36,579 --> 00:15:40,840
that is not conductive to them developing as a leader,

232
00:15:41,439 --> 00:15:43,459
they're just not going to be involved.

233
00:15:44,560 --> 00:15:47,560
Once we recognize the slightest gesture of initiative

234
00:15:47,560 --> 00:15:49,540
on the part of any of our scouts,

235
00:15:49,939 --> 00:15:52,860
we need to respond with unalloyed encouragement.

236
00:15:54,079 --> 00:15:55,220
And each time we do,

237
00:15:55,220 --> 00:15:57,439
they will take successively bigger steps

238
00:15:57,440 --> 00:16:01,060
and soon become the competent leaders that we're looking for.

239
00:16:01,260 --> 00:16:05,180
887, www.labbilitystations.com

240
00:16:05,180 --> 00:16:06,079
So that's all we talk about.

241
00:16:06,079 --> 00:16:07,040
So,

242
00:16:07,040 --> 00:16:07,180
to begin,

243
00:16:07,180 --> 00:16:08,140
break someone's perspective

244
00:16:10,199 --> 00:16:10,900
and start to jump in and start

245
00:16:12,199 --> 00:16:14,099
everything in a moment of a career

246
00:16:17,640 --> 00:16:17,839
or taking progress

247
00:16:17,839 --> 00:16:19,060
or missing a piece to these religions

248
00:16:19,060 --> 00:16:20,380
can't reveal anything we see

249
00:16:20,380 --> 00:16:21,719
for the whole Times.

250
00:16:22,459 --> 00:16:24,560
And all that's it.

251
00:16:24,560 --> 00:16:44,439
Hi, I'm Kevin Callen, the Happy Camper, and you're listening to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

252
00:16:44,439 --> 00:16:48,939
Write me a letter, send it by mail.

253
00:16:50,459 --> 00:16:52,040
Email, that is, folks.

254
00:16:52,040 --> 00:16:55,640
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

255
00:17:02,860 --> 00:17:07,639
So Ray wrote in about the discussion we had of patrol ages in the last podcast.

256
00:17:07,879 --> 00:17:08,760
This is what he had to say.

257
00:17:09,060 --> 00:17:13,079
Another excellent podcast, the discussion of patrol ages is very timely.

258
00:17:13,579 --> 00:17:18,079
As we enter the season of Weeblos crossing over into Boy Scouts, I've been considering our patrols.

259
00:17:18,079 --> 00:17:23,060
We have used the new Scout patrol, and after a year, these Scouts get integrated into other patrols.

260
00:17:23,159 --> 00:17:27,679
We also shift other Scouts around to balance patrols as the older Scouts move on.

261
00:17:28,179 --> 00:17:32,260
I've been considering the merits of distributing new Scouts into the existing patrols.

262
00:17:32,260 --> 00:17:40,180
First, I thought I had read that the original intent was that the patrol would form and be a permanent patrol that would not be changed too frequently to allow the patrol to fully develop.

263
00:17:40,820 --> 00:17:43,920
New Scouts would flow into the patrol, and older Scouts would flow out.

264
00:17:44,280 --> 00:17:48,520
The patrol makeup would change through the aging process, not by shuffling the members.

265
00:17:49,200 --> 00:17:54,400
Of course, as the troop grows or shrinks, the age distribution of a patrol may get unbalanced.

266
00:17:54,920 --> 00:17:58,480
Now, this might not be a big deal, but I have three questions for you.

267
00:17:58,480 --> 00:18:07,100
First, I'm interested in your thoughts about changes in patrol makeup and how these changes affect the patrol performance and the goals of Scouting.

268
00:18:07,840 --> 00:18:19,560
Well, before I answer that first question, I want to give a tip of the campaign hat to Larry Geiger, who is a frequent contributor to the podcast and a commenter on the blog.

269
00:18:20,120 --> 00:18:27,880
He offered some comments to raise questions on the blog, and I and Larry are pretty much on the same page about this.

270
00:18:27,880 --> 00:18:40,260
I get a lot of questions about the new Scout Patrol and the way patrols are described in our training materials, because the way I do this and what I say and write differs from the advice offered in training.

271
00:18:41,140 --> 00:18:48,120
So what I want to do is concentrate on the broad underlying concepts of Scouting that bear on the issue of patrols.

272
00:18:48,760 --> 00:18:51,260
Boys join Scouting to do great stuff with their friends.

273
00:18:51,260 --> 00:18:55,980
To my mind, they're the best judge of who their friends are, not me.

274
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:02,000
In some practices in Scouting are adopted because, frankly, they drive good numbers.

275
00:19:02,540 --> 00:19:03,920
They make for good statistics.

276
00:19:04,800 --> 00:19:08,520
We want to retain more Scouts, and we want more of them to advance in ranks.

277
00:19:09,140 --> 00:19:16,080
The new Scout Patrol was implemented because it was found that boys who attained first class rank in the first year were more likely to stay in Scouting.

278
00:19:16,080 --> 00:19:19,100
I mean, that's what the statistics showed.

279
00:19:19,560 --> 00:19:22,580
So the new Scout Patrol would be a good way to make this happen.

280
00:19:23,679 --> 00:19:31,000
There are also concerns, you know, about younger boys being intimidated by older boys and leaving Scouts, and that they would be more comfortable with their peers.

281
00:19:31,960 --> 00:19:35,820
Now, this all sounds great, and I'm sure it's effective in attaining goals for which it's intended,

282
00:19:36,020 --> 00:19:40,399
but it also had some unintentional effects, this new Scout Patrol plan.

283
00:19:40,400 --> 00:19:47,960
Perhaps the most deleterious one is that Scoutmasters think they need to engineer patrol memberships to get a certain result.

284
00:19:49,100 --> 00:19:54,460
So common practice has become that Scoutmasters are making decisions about who goes in what patrol.

285
00:19:55,300 --> 00:20:01,980
And once you start doing this, you end up pretty much continuously re-engineering and maintaining patrols.

286
00:20:01,980 --> 00:20:07,600
This is compounded by the fact that adults have an aversion to unpredictable results

287
00:20:07,600 --> 00:20:12,520
and the kind of uncertainties that follows when boys make decisions about these things.

288
00:20:13,260 --> 00:20:19,600
As I said, I think my Scouts are much better judges of who their friends are and who they want to do Scouting with in their patrol,

289
00:20:20,280 --> 00:20:22,320
and I'd rather that they make these decisions.

290
00:20:23,280 --> 00:20:25,680
There's also the idea of a permanent patrol,

291
00:20:25,680 --> 00:20:31,720
one that exists in perpetuity and just changes members as the Scouts age in and out of the patrol.

292
00:20:32,560 --> 00:20:34,040
I think it's great when that happens,

293
00:20:34,140 --> 00:20:37,180
but I wouldn't engineer patrols specifically to make that happen.

294
00:20:38,680 --> 00:20:39,940
Really, what's more important?

295
00:20:40,380 --> 00:20:42,580
A Scout being happy and working along with his buddies,

296
00:20:42,840 --> 00:20:44,140
or having a permanent patrol?

297
00:20:44,680 --> 00:20:47,880
I'd rather have a happy Scout working with his friends.

298
00:20:48,560 --> 00:20:51,280
Some patrols end up lasting for years and years.

299
00:20:51,400 --> 00:20:53,900
They change members but maintain the same name,

300
00:20:54,380 --> 00:20:55,140
and that's great.

301
00:20:55,140 --> 00:20:58,360
But if that doesn't happen, I don't think that's any big deal.

302
00:20:58,580 --> 00:21:00,020
So what's the broad underlying concept?

303
00:21:00,160 --> 00:21:01,160
Well, I'll repeat it again.

304
00:21:01,420 --> 00:21:05,600
Scouts join Scouts because they want to do the things that Scouts do with their friends.

305
00:21:06,360 --> 00:21:08,600
If they're happy doing this with the boys they choose,

306
00:21:08,720 --> 00:21:11,000
they'll advance, they'll develop some skills in leadership,

307
00:21:11,620 --> 00:21:13,920
they'll develop some practical camping and Scout skills,

308
00:21:14,100 --> 00:21:15,840
and they'll learn to work together to get things done.

309
00:21:15,920 --> 00:21:17,500
It's a very natural process.

310
00:21:18,280 --> 00:21:19,860
They won't do these things on a schedule,

311
00:21:20,020 --> 00:21:22,780
they won't wring every ounce of potential out of every opportunity,

312
00:21:22,780 --> 00:21:25,700
but they will be doing something for themselves.

313
00:21:25,879 --> 00:21:27,940
And really, that's what we're all about.

314
00:21:27,940 --> 00:21:30,820
The second question is the maintenance of patrols.

315
00:21:32,160 --> 00:21:35,660
And I understand you leave the patrol organization up to your patrol leader's council.

316
00:21:36,280 --> 00:21:39,480
Do you give some guidance to the patrol leader's council about the outcome?

317
00:21:40,740 --> 00:21:44,400
Patrols should have some balance either in patrol size or age distribution.

318
00:21:45,160 --> 00:21:48,380
Well, I do keep this subject in front of my senior patrol leader all the time.

319
00:21:48,380 --> 00:21:54,500
I train him to continuously monitor his patrols and to be aware of when changes should be made.

320
00:21:55,520 --> 00:21:59,020
My direction to him is that patrols really need to have six to eight Scouts,

321
00:21:59,500 --> 00:22:03,500
and they need to function on their own without undue oversight from adults or youth leaders.

322
00:22:04,240 --> 00:22:07,220
I also counsel them that stability is a good thing,

323
00:22:07,340 --> 00:22:10,600
and they should think through any changes they're going to make carefully.

324
00:22:11,720 --> 00:22:13,640
You know, frankly, they do a great job of this.

325
00:22:13,640 --> 00:22:18,800
I don't want my assistant scoutmasters involved with patrols at all,

326
00:22:18,920 --> 00:22:24,640
unless it's to assist with the occasional specific request from the senior patrol leader or patrol leader.

327
00:22:25,180 --> 00:22:29,800
I don't want my youth leaders to do the patrol leader's job or interfere in any way

328
00:22:29,800 --> 00:22:31,880
or kind of micromanage him.

329
00:22:32,420 --> 00:22:36,060
I want them to give the same latitude that I give them.

330
00:22:36,620 --> 00:22:39,560
So that's, you know, I give them some very basic advice,

331
00:22:39,560 --> 00:22:43,760
and I kind of monitor what they're doing, and I leave them to it.

332
00:22:44,879 --> 00:22:50,899
The third question, do you move older Scouts out of a regular patrol into a venture patrol,

333
00:22:51,139 --> 00:22:53,360
and what's the criterion for making that transition?

334
00:22:53,600 --> 00:22:56,120
We've done this a couple of different ways over the years,

335
00:22:56,159 --> 00:22:59,200
and this seems to be the most workable solution that I've found so far.

336
00:22:59,399 --> 00:23:04,899
We do have a venture crew that includes every Scout in the troop who's 13 or older.

337
00:23:04,900 --> 00:23:11,020
Now, this venture crew has not been set up or officially designated in paperwork or anything like that.

338
00:23:11,100 --> 00:23:16,420
This is just a handy handle that we hang on this group of boys who are 13 and older.

339
00:23:16,900 --> 00:23:22,220
But that crew only exists when we have a venture activity planned,

340
00:23:22,280 --> 00:23:23,780
and that's usually about once a year.

341
00:23:23,960 --> 00:23:26,340
They're not a regular patrol in the troop.

342
00:23:26,940 --> 00:23:28,900
They're all in their own patrols.

343
00:23:29,040 --> 00:23:32,740
When it's time to start talking about the venture activity that we're going to do,

344
00:23:32,740 --> 00:23:34,640
the big trip or something like that,

345
00:23:34,940 --> 00:23:41,540
then they will get together outside of a meeting, you know, a troop meeting or anything like that.

346
00:23:41,620 --> 00:23:47,420
They'll elect a crew chief, and they'll begin their planning and execution of whatever trip they want to do,

347
00:23:47,900 --> 00:23:49,340
much like a patrol would.

348
00:23:50,280 --> 00:23:52,060
We just end up calling it a venture crew.

349
00:23:53,320 --> 00:23:58,060
My senior patrol leader, my assistant senior patrol leader, the scribe and the quartermaster,

350
00:23:58,060 --> 00:24:04,919
and any junior assistant scoutmasters we have at the time are the only youth members who are not attached to a regular patrol.

351
00:24:05,340 --> 00:24:12,520
They function as a patrol for outings along with the adults who are attending in that they plan meals together and things like that.

352
00:24:12,800 --> 00:24:18,720
Other than that, you know, these guys, these three or four youth leaders are busy running the show.

353
00:24:19,520 --> 00:24:25,879
So naturally the question comes, well, so if a guy is no longer one of those four guys, what does he do?

354
00:24:25,880 --> 00:24:29,880
Does he go back to a patrol? Usually he's old enough that he's going to be appointed.

355
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,420
He's going to be 16, 17 years old at that point.

356
00:24:32,500 --> 00:24:38,360
He's going to be appointed to be a junior assistant scoutmaster and kind of work in with that older boy group.

357
00:24:39,500 --> 00:24:44,960
But there have been instances where a guy has left one of those higher leadership positions, goes back and works with a patrol.

358
00:24:45,460 --> 00:24:46,380
Hey, no problem.

359
00:24:46,760 --> 00:24:49,920
I would encourage you to look at the patrol method book if you haven't already.

360
00:24:50,380 --> 00:24:53,700
We talked a little bit about how to find that in the beginning of the podcast.

361
00:24:53,700 --> 00:24:57,940
But I'll tell you one more time, scoutmaster.typepad.com.

362
00:24:58,540 --> 00:25:00,320
Look over at the right-hand column.

363
00:25:00,800 --> 00:25:02,140
You'll see the patrol method book.

364
00:25:02,740 --> 00:25:07,320
And you'll see how to be able to purchase one, my review, and the interview that I did with the authors.

365
00:25:07,660 --> 00:25:10,060
So, Ray, I appreciate the questions, appreciate the comments.

366
00:25:10,180 --> 00:25:13,100
And, Larry, sure appreciate the input and helping me answer.

367
00:25:13,100 --> 00:25:31,580
Well, thanks for listening.

368
00:25:31,580 --> 00:25:34,620
And here's how you can keep in touch.

369
00:25:35,300 --> 00:25:39,919
You can read the scoutmaster blog at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

370
00:25:39,920 --> 00:25:49,060
And when you go, you can subscribe to the Scoutmaster newsletter, which is a monthly digest of the Scoutmaster blog and podcast that you'll receive by email.

371
00:25:49,560 --> 00:25:55,220
And it's always going to be designed around some good scouting resources around a monthly theme.

372
00:25:55,220 --> 00:25:57,980
January's newsletter has already gone out.

373
00:25:58,280 --> 00:26:03,760
But if you would like to get on on the next one, do go to scoutmaster.typepad.com.

374
00:26:03,840 --> 00:26:05,020
Look in the upper right-hand corner.

375
00:26:05,120 --> 00:26:07,400
And you'll see how you can subscribe to the newsletter.

376
00:26:08,040 --> 00:26:11,240
Every newsletter includes a giveaway from one of our affiliate sponsors.

377
00:26:11,420 --> 00:26:15,700
And this month, in January, we're giving away a $50 gift certificate to the CLIMB.

378
00:26:16,180 --> 00:26:19,060
C-L-Y-M-B is the way they spell CLIMB.

379
00:26:19,060 --> 00:26:23,800
And it's a great discount house for camping gear and camping apparel.

380
00:26:32,179 --> 00:26:35,260
You can subscribe to the Scoutmaster podcast on iTunes.

381
00:26:35,399 --> 00:26:38,780
And when you do, feel free to leave a comment or a review or a rating.

382
00:26:39,300 --> 00:26:41,139
And there's so many ways you can follow us.

383
00:26:41,200 --> 00:26:45,980
You can follow us at Scoutmaster Blog on Facebook and ScoutmasterCG on Twitter.

384
00:26:45,980 --> 00:26:53,800
And you can also email me, Clark Green, with your comments and questions at ClarkGreen at gmail.com.

385
00:26:54,220 --> 00:26:59,020
That's C-L-A-R-K-E-G-R-E-E-N at gmail.com.

386
00:26:59,020 --> 00:27:17,620
As always, you must remember the Scoutmaster Blog and the Scoutmaster Podcast are not official publications of the Boy Scouts of America.

387
00:27:18,200 --> 00:27:18,960
Oh, no, no, no.

388
00:27:19,580 --> 00:27:22,480
Nor are they sponsored or endorsed by the Boy Scouts of America.

389
00:27:23,040 --> 00:27:27,960
Hey, it's just me with a microphone, trying to lend a hand to Scout leaders and perhaps have a little bit of fun along.

390
00:27:27,960 --> 00:27:28,960
Thank you.

391
00:27:35,140 --> 00:27:37,520
So, before we leave you, we want to hear from...

392
00:27:37,520 --> 00:27:39,740
I don't have to show you any stinking watches.

393
00:27:39,900 --> 00:27:41,340
No, we don't want to hear from you.

394
00:27:41,860 --> 00:27:42,960
We've heard from you already.

395
00:27:43,700 --> 00:27:47,100
Thanks very much, but, you know, service is no longer required.

396
00:27:48,540 --> 00:27:52,940
No, we want to hear from the founder of Scouting, Sir Robert Baden-Powell.

397
00:27:53,180 --> 00:27:53,640
Sir Robert?

398
00:27:54,540 --> 00:27:56,740
Good luck to you and good camping.

399
00:27:56,740 --> 00:27:57,580
Thank you, sir.

400
00:27:58,620 --> 00:27:59,620
Until next time.

401
00:27:59,620 --> 00:28:04,260
I don't solve thisbing, sir.

402
00:28:04,580 --> 00:28:05,000
I don't want to hear from you.

403
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:05,620
Her name is Noreen from the Girl.

404
00:28:06,800 --> 00:28:09,139
And if you are now, it may have a favorite should be safe.

405
00:28:09,139 --> 00:28:10,879
Perhaps I have a hospital on every either.

406
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:16,820
If your neces

407
00:28:20,879 --> 00:28:26,600
For my health and has been closed Portland, it may have a profesional experience.

408
00:28:26,600 --> 00:28:48,320
Thank you.

409
00:28:56,600 --> 00:29:26,580
Thank you.

